      "description": "The Adhafer-class corvette is a stealth-designed vessel built for the Algerian Navy by the China State Shipbuilding Corporation (CSSC) at the Hudong-Zhonghua shipyard in Shanghai. Algeria ordered three vessels of the class, with an option for three additional units. The lead ship, Adhafer , was launched in August 2014 and entered service in November 2015. The remaining two units, El Fateh and Ezzadjer , entered service in 2016. CSSC subsequently displayed a model of an updated variant of the design in late 2016, featuring active electronically scanned array (AESA) radar technology and revised weapon configurations.\n\nThe hull design incorporates radar-absorbing paint and a low-profile structure to minimize radar signature. Propulsion is provided by MTU diesel engines, which exhaust near the waterline rather than through a traditional funnel stack, reducing the vessel's infrared signature. The hull is fitted with bilge keels and fin stabilizers. The ship's combat system integrates a Chinese combat management system with Thales-designed radar systems, operator consoles, and datalinks.\n\nThe armament suite consists of a main gun housed in a reduced radar cross-section turret, short-range air defense missiles, anti-ship missiles, torpedo launchers, and close-in weapon systems (CIWS) mounted on the hangar. Electronic warfare capabilities include an ESM system and decoy launchers that deploy chaff, launch anti-submarine rockets, or provide shore bombardment. Sensor systems comprise a multibeam surveillance radar, navigation radars, targeting and secondary air search radars, specialized fire-control radars, and a hull-mounted sonar.\n\nThe three completed vessels— Adhafer (920), El Fateh (921), and Ezzadjer (922)—are currently active within the Algerian Navy.",
