      "description": "Cantabria (A15) is a replenishment oiler operated by the Spanish Navy. Designed by Navantia, the vessel was laid down at the Puerto Real shipyard in Cadiz on 18 July 2007. It was launched on 21 July 2008, completed sea trials in the Bay of Cadiz, and entered service on 29 September 2010. The design was also marketed for export; while Canada and Norway evaluated the design but chose alternative options, Australia selected the Cantabria design in March 2016 to serve as the basis for its two Supply -class replenishment vessels.\n\nClassified in Spain as a Buque de Aprovisionamiento en Combate ( Supply Ship in Combat), the vessel provides logistical support during fleet operations, expeditionary deployments, and humanitarian or disaster relief missions. Propulsion is provided by two diesel engines driving a single propeller shaft fitted with a controllable-pitch propeller. The ship features double-hulled fuel storage areas and can transport ship fuel, jet fuel, fresh water, ammunition, and general cargo. It is equipped to replenish three ships simultaneously, using stations on each side and at the stern. For vertical replenishment operations, the vessel has a flight deck and hangar capable of supporting Agusta-Bell AB.212, Sikorsky SH-3 Sea King , or NHIndustries NH90 helicopters.\n\nIn July 2012, Cantabria participated in exercise MAR-22 off the Galician coast alongside Spanish frigates, a submarine, and naval aircraft. Under an agreement between the Spanish and Australian governments, the ship deployed to Australian waters from February to November 2013 to provide logistical support while the Australian oiler HMAS Success underwent maintenance. During this deployment, Cantabria performed 63 replenishments, participated in the International Fleet Review 2013, and provided training for over 300 Australian personnel on systems similar to those on the Hobart and Canberra-class vessels then under acquisition. In 2016, under a separate bilateral agreement, the vessel deployed to support training missions with the Royal Canadian Navy from mid-September to November.",
