      "description": "The submarine Hai Shih (S 791) was originally built for the United States Navy as the Tench -class vessel USS Cutlass (SS-473). Launched in 1944, the submarine belongs to a class developed as an improvement over the preceding Gato and Balao designs, featuring a stronger physical construction and a modified internal layout that converted a ballast tank to carry fuel for increased range.\n\nThe design of the class resolved a safety vulnerability present in earlier submarines by rearranging the main ballast tanks to remove internal vent riser pipes from the torpedo rooms. This change eliminated the risk of high-pressure pipe ruptures during depth charge attacks and freed internal space, allowing the vessel to carry additional torpedoes. The original diesel-electric propulsion system utilized diesel engines supplying direct-drive electric motors to turn two shafts, with lead-acid batteries providing submerged power.\n\nBetween 1947 and 1949, the submarine underwent a GUPPY II modernization. This configuration replaced the original batteries with more compact lead-acid units, increasing the total battery cell count to improve submerged power. The upgrade also added a snorkel, streamlined the sail and bow, upgraded the electric motors to double-armature types, and updated the electrical, air conditioning, sonar, fire-control, and electronic support measures systems.\n\nThe submarine entered Japanese waters for its first war patrol immediately after the August 1945 ceasefire. Following its post-war GUPPY II conversion, the vessel continued in United States Navy service until 1973, when it was transferred to the Republic of China Navy of Taiwan and renamed Hai Shih . Both boats remain in service with the 256th Submarine Squadron, the last operational World War II-era, US-built submarines in service with any navy. Retirement is tied to the entry into service of the domestically built Hai Kun , which has not been commissioned after missing a November 2025 delivery deadline; the plan is to withdraw whichever of the two boats has the lower readiness first and retain the other as a training boat. The class carried the largest weapons load of any conventional submarine then in service.",
