      "description": "The Nilgiri class, formally Project 17A, is a series of seven stealth guided-missile frigates built for the Indian Navy as an evolution of the Shivalik class (Project 17). Designed by the Navy's Warship Design Bureau with a significantly reduced radar cross-section and infrared signature, the ships are split between Mazagon Dock in Mumbai (four) and Garden Reach in Kolkata (three) — the largest Indian frigate programme ever run in parallel at two yards.\n\nAt 6,670 tons and 149 meters, the ships are driven by a CODOG plant pairing two LM2500 gas turbines with diesels for 32 knots. The armament is heavily indigenised: eight BrahMos supersonic cruise missiles in vertical launchers, thirty-two Barak-8 (MRSAM) air-defence missiles, a 76 mm Oto Melara gun, two AK-630M close-in weapon systems, torpedo launchers and anti-submarine rocket launchers, plus a hangar for a Dhruv or Sea King helicopter, with the MH-60R entering the fleet.\n\nThe lead ship Nilgiri commissioned in January 2025 — alongside a destroyer and a submarine in a single ceremony showcasing Indian shipbuilding — and deliveries have proceeded at a rapid clip, with six ships in service by mid-2026 and the seventh, Mahendragiri, completing. The class revives the names of the Indian Navy's first-generation Nilgiri-class ( Leander ) frigates and will be followed by the further-improved Project 17B.",
